Hi Nistara

Thanks for the links, that's interesting! It looks like the functionalities to create a sequential palette from 2 colors is actually available from within R, with colorRampPalette (or chroma package which copies the choma.js the link was talking about). Trying this for the sequential palette gave poor results (see first palette in graph), I guess the colours are too close from each other. I tried adding a third colour and it is slightly better.

And for the qualitative one, it turns out that the "Secondary colours" provided on the Davis colour style seem to align quite nicely with the primary colours at 60%. I'll let anyone judge!

Thanks

Matthieu

#devtools::install_github("jiho/chroma")
library("chroma")
library("ggplot2")
chroma::show_col(
  interp_colors(7, c("#002855", "#DAAA00")),
  interp_colors(7, c("#002855","#4F2C1D",  "#DAAA00")),
  interp_colors(7, c("#002855","#5B7F95",  "#DAAA00")),
  interp_colors(7, c("#002855","#00573F",  "#DAAA00"))
)

cols <- c(orange_hartHall="#E6A65D",
          grey_centennial="#B1B3B3",
          red_MU_brick = "#C26E60",
          green_sage ="#9CAF88",
          yellow_60="#E9CC66",
          blue_60="#667D9D")

seq_cols <- seq_len(length(cols))

qplot(x=seq_cols, y= seq_cols, geom="col", fill= names(cols)) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = cols)

